module

import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.NumberTheory.NumberField.Completion.FinitePlace
public import Mathlib.NumberTheory.NumberField.Completion.InfinitePlace
public import Mathlib.RepresentationTheory.Homological.ContCohomology.Sha
public import MathlibExt.AlgebraicGeometry.EllipticCurve.PointGaloisAction

/-! # The Cassels–Tate pairing for elliptic curves over number fields

A statement-only request for Cassels' theorem: the Shafarevich–Tate group of an elliptic curve
over a number field carries an alternating pairing to $\mathbb{Q}/\mathbb{Z}$ that is
nondegenerate modulo the maximal divisible subgroup.

The Shafarevich–Tate group is Mathlib's `ContinuousCohomology.tateShafarevich` in degree one,
for the Galois module $E(\bar K)$ of nonsingular points of $E$ over an algebraic closure $\bar K$
(all of its points when $E$ is elliptic) with the discrete topology, localized at every place of
$K$: the finite places through `IsDedekindDomain.HeightOneSpectrum.adicCompletion` and the
infinite places through `NumberField.InfinitePlace.Completion`. As explained in the
implementation notes of `Mathlib.RepresentationTheory.Homological.ContCohomology.Sha`, the local
groups there are $H^1(G_{K_v}, E(\bar K))$ rather than $H^1(G_{K_v}, E(\bar K_v))$, where
$G_{K_v}$ acts on $\bar K$ through a $K$-embedding of $\bar K$ into an algebraic closure
$\bar K_v$ of $K_v$.

When $E$ is elliptic (equivalently, $\Delta(E) \ne 0$), the map
$H^1(G_{K_v}, E(\bar K)) \to H^1(G_{K_v}, E(\bar K_v))$ induced by that embedding is injective
at every place $v$, so both choices of local groups cut out the same subgroup of
$H^1(G_K, E(\bar K))$, the Shafarevich–Tate group of $E$:

* at a finite place, restriction identifies $G_{K_v}$ with the Galois group of $\bar K$ over the
  henselization $K_v^h$ of $K$ at $v$, and the map is the isomorphism
  $H^1(K_v^h, E) \to H^1(K_v, E)$ of Milne, *Arithmetic Duality Theorems*, 2nd ed.,
  Remark I.3.10 (a)(ii), p. 47, which applies as $E$ is an abelian variety;
* at a real place, $G_{K_v}$ is generated by a complex conjugation whose fixed field $F$ in
  $\bar K$ is real closed, with $\bar K = F(\sqrt{-1})$, and the map is
  $H^1(F, E) \to H^1(\mathbb{R}, E)$; it is injective because a principal homogeneous space of
  the elliptic curve $E$ over $F$ with a real point has a point over $F$, by Tarski's transfer
  principle for real closed fields;
* at a complex place, $G_{K_v}$ is trivial and both groups are zero.
-/

namespace MetaMathlibExt

@[expose] public section

open scoped WeierstrassCurve.Affine

open NumberField IsDedekindDomain

variable {K : Type} [Field K]

open scoped Classical in
/-- **The Galois module of points of an elliptic curve.** Let $E$ be a Weierstrass curve over a
field $K$ with algebraic closure $\bar K$. This is the group $E(\bar K)$ of nonsingular points
of $E$ with coordinates in $\bar K$ (the point at infinity and the nonsingular affine points, so
all points when $E$ is elliptic), with the discrete topology, as a topological
$\mathbb{Z}$-representation of the absolute Galois group $G_K = \mathrm{Gal}(\bar K/K)$, which
acts on the coordinates of points. -/
noncomputable def ellipticPointsTopRep (E : WeierstrassCurve K) :
    TopRep ℤ (Field.absoluteGaloisGroup K) :=
  letI : TopologicalSpace (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure K)).Point := ⊥
  haveI : DiscreteTopology (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure K)).Point := ⟨rfl⟩
  TopRep.of (X := (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure K)).Point)
    (ContRepresentation.ofMonoidHom
      { toFun := fun σ : AlgebraicClosure K ≃ₐ[K] AlgebraicClosure K =>
          ⟨(DistribSMul.toAddMonoidHom (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure K)).Point σ).toIntLinearMap,
            continuous_of_discreteTopology⟩
        map_one' := by
          ext P
          exact one_smul (AlgebraicClosure K ≃ₐ[K] AlgebraicClosure K) P
        map_mul' := fun σ τ => by
          ext P
          exact mul_smul (α := AlgebraicClosure K ≃ₐ[K] AlgebraicClosure K) σ τ P })

/-- **The Shafarevich–Tate group of an elliptic curve over a number field.** Let $E$ be a
Weierstrass curve over a number field $K$ with algebraic closure $\bar K$, and let $E(\bar K)$ be
the group of nonsingular points of $E$ with coordinates in $\bar K$. This is the subgroup of the
continuous cohomology group $H^1(G_K, E(\bar K))$ of classes whose restriction to the absolute
Galois group $G_{K_v}$ of the completion $K_v$ is zero for every place $v$ of $K$, finite or
infinite.

Here $G_{K_v}$ acts on $\bar K$ through a $K$-embedding of $\bar K$ into an algebraic closure
$\bar K_v$ of $K_v$, and the restriction lies in $H^1(G_{K_v}, E(\bar K))$, with the coefficients
$E(\bar K)$ fixed. When $E$ is elliptic (equivalently, $\Delta(E) \ne 0$), this subgroup is the
Shafarevich–Tate group $\text{Ш}(E/K)$: the map from $H^1(G_{K_v}, E(\bar K))$ to
$H^1(G_{K_v}, E(\bar K_v))$ is injective at every place, so the subgroup is the same as the one
defined with the local groups $H^1(G_{K_v}, E(\bar K_v))$. At a finite place the map is an
isomorphism (Milne, *Arithmetic Duality Theorems*, 2nd ed., Remark I.3.10 (a)(ii), p. 47, for the
abelian variety $E$ over the henselization of $K$ at $v$), at a real place it is injective by
Tarski's transfer principle for real closed fields, and at a complex place both groups are
zero. -/
noncomputable def ellipticTateShafarevich [NumberField K] (E : WeierstrassCurve K) :
    AddSubgroup (continuousCohomology 1 (ellipticPointsTopRep E)) :=
  ContinuousCohomology.tateShafarevich
      (fun v : HeightOneSpectrum (𝓞 K) => v.adicCompletion K) (ellipticPointsTopRep E) 1 ⊓
    ContinuousCohomology.tateShafarevich (fun w : InfinitePlace K => w.Completion)
      (ellipticPointsTopRep E) 1

/-- **Cassels' pairing on the Shafarevich–Tate group of an elliptic curve.** Let $E$ be an
elliptic curve over a number field $K$, and let $\text{Ш}(E)$ be its Shafarevich–Tate group.
There is a biadditive pairing
$\langle\,,\rangle \colon \text{Ш}(E) \times \text{Ш}(E) \to \mathbb{Q}/\mathbb{Z}$ that is
alternating, $\langle x, x\rangle = 0$ for all $x$, and that becomes nondegenerate after one
divides $\text{Ш}(E)$ by its maximal divisible subgroup: an element $x$ satisfies
$\langle x, y\rangle = 0$ for all $y$ if and only if $x$ lies in a divisible subgroup of
$\text{Ш}(E)$.

The divisible subgroups generate a divisible subgroup, so an element lies in some divisible
subgroup exactly when it lies in the maximal divisible subgroup. The kernel on the other side is
the same, since an alternating pairing is antisymmetric. The statement asserts that such a
pairing exists; it does not single out the pairing Cassels constructs.

Source: B. Poonen and M. Stoll, *The Cassels-Tate pairing on polarized abelian varieties*,
Ann. of Math. (2) 150 (1999), 1109–1149, doi:10.2307/121064, Introduction, p. 1110, stating the
theorem of J. W. S. Cassels, *Arithmetic on curves of genus 1. IV. Proof of the
Hauptvermutung*, J. Reine Angew. Math. 211 (1962), 95–112. See also J. S. Milne,
*Arithmetic Duality Theorems*, 2nd ed. (2006), Theorem I.6.13 (a), p. 82, for the kernels, and
p. 81 for the alternating property. -/
theorem_wanted cassels_tate_pairing [NumberField K] (E : WeierstrassCurve K) [E.IsElliptic] :
    ∃ b : ellipticTateShafarevich E →+ ellipticTateShafarevich E →+
        (ℚ ⧸ AddSubgroup.zmultiples (1 : ℚ)),
      (∀ x, b x x = 0) ∧
        ∀ x, (∀ y, b x y = 0) ↔
          ∃ H : AddSubgroup (ellipticTateShafarevich E),
            (∀ h ∈ H, ∀ n : ℕ, n ≠ 0 → ∃ g ∈ H, n • g = h) ∧ x ∈ H

end

end MetaMathlibExt
